/* ==============================================================
   06 — Blending: cycle time you get for free
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   SetBlending
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, no tool mounted
   WATCH     The same five-point path, stop-and-go then flowing
   TIME      ~35 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE COMMAND

       SetBlending(p)     p from 0 (off) to 100 (default)

   By default it is already on, at 100%. Most people discover
   blending by turning it OFF and wondering why the robot got
   slow and jerky.

   WHAT IT DOES

   Without blending, the robot comes to a complete stop at every
   waypoint before starting the next move. Ten waypoints means
   ten full decelerations and ten full accelerations, and all of
   that time is spent going nowhere.

   With blending, the robot rounds the corner: it begins the next
   move before finishing the current one, cutting the corner
   smoothly instead of stopping on it.

   The result is a path that flows, finishes measurably sooner,
   and puts far less shock through the mechanism and the payload.

   THE TRADE-OFF, STATED HONESTLY

   A blended corner DOES NOT PASS THROUGH THE WAYPOINT. It passes
   near it. That is the entire cost, and it is the first question
   a good integrator will ask.

   So:
     - Blend the via points, the corners you only put in to get
       around something. Nobody cares about the exact position of
       a mid-air waypoint.
     - Do not blend a point where the robot does work. Turn
       blending off, or place a full stop, wherever the tool must
       actually arrive: the pick, the place, the start of a bead.

   The usual pattern is blending on for the whole program, with
   the process points naturally isolated because the robot has to
   stop there anyway to grip, dispense, or wait.

   HOW MUCH IS 100%?

   100% means blending occupies the whole of the acceleration and
   deceleration phases — the roundest corner available. Lower
   values cut the corner less and stay closer to the waypoint,
   which makes a direct precision-versus-speed dial. Values
   around 20 to 50 are a reasonable middle when a path has to
   stay reasonably close to its via points.

   Note that blending is reset to its default on every activation,
   and that using SetCartAcc forces a full stop between moves
   even when blending is enabled.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(40)
SetCartLinVel(200)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
MovePose(190, -70, 300, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Blending off: stop at every corner ------------------------
   Run this first. It is the "before" picture, and it is what a
   naive program does. Count the stops — there are four. */
SetBlending(0)

MoveLin(190, -70, 250, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 0, 250, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 0, 300, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 70, 300, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 70, 250, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(2)


// --- Reset for the second pass --------------------------------
MovePose(190, -70, 300, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Blending on: one continuous motion ------------------------
   The five motion lines below are identical to the five above.
   Only SetBlending changed. The path now flows through the
   corners as a single gesture, and finishes noticeably sooner.

   Time both passes with a phone if you want the number. On a
   path like this the difference is usually large enough that
   nobody asks for the stopwatch. */
SetBlending(100)

MoveLin(190, -70, 250, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 0, 250, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 0, 300, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 70, 300, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 70, 250, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(2)


// --- Reset for the third pass ---------------------------------
MovePose(190, -70, 300, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Partial blending: the dial --------------------------------
   Same path again at 25%. Corners are rounded, but much less —
   the path stays close to the waypoints while still avoiding
   the dead stops. This is the setting to reach for when the via
   points matter somewhat, but not exactly. */
SetBlending(25)

MoveLin(190, -70, 250, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 0, 250, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 0, 300, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 70, 300, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, 70, 250, 0, 90, 0)


/* --- The real-world pattern ------------------------------------
   Blending on for travel, off for the point that matters. */
SetBlending(100)
MovePose(190, 40, 290, 0, 90, 0)      // via, blended
MovePose(190, 0, 280, 0, 90, 0)       // via, blended

SetBlending(0)
MoveLin(190, 0, 250, 0, 90, 0)        // the actual work point
Delay(0.5)                            // arrives exactly here
MoveLin(190, 0, 280, 0, 90, 0)

SetBlending(100)


// --- Home -----------------------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Run pass one and pass two back to back and say nothing.
      This is a rare demo where the improvement is obvious to
      someone with no robotics background at all.

   2. If they ask about accuracy — and the good ones do — answer
      directly: the blended path does not pass through the
      waypoint. Then show the last block, where the work point
      has blending turned off. Being straight about the trade-off
      is worth more than the extra second of cycle time.

   NEXT   07_gripper.mxprog — actually picking something up.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
